## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects/functions compare by reference — a new reference every render means a new prop, even if the data is identical.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child on every Parent render — new object reference each time
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
}
```

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | Component output | Child receives stable props but re-renders due to parent |
| `useMemo` | Computed value | Expensive calculation, or object passed as prop/context value |
| `useCallback` | Function reference | Function passed to memoized child or used as effect dependency |

**Don't reach for these by default.** Memoization has its own cost — it only pays off when the thing being skipped is measurably expensive or causes a cascade.

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context *value reference* changes. The most common mistake is constructing the value inline:

```jsx
// Bad — new object every render, all consumers re-render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Fix with `useMemo`:
```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
return <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AuthContext.Provider>;
```

Or split into two contexts — one for state, one for stable dispatch — so setters don't trigger re-renders in state-only consumers.

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke because `filters` is a new object and `onSelect` is a new function each render:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        filters={{ query, limit: 50 }}   // new object every render
        onSelect={id => setSelected(id)} // new function every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ filters, onSelect }) => { /* ... */ };
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `query` or `selected` actually changes:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);

  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ query, limit: 50 }), [query]);
  const onSelect = useCallback(id => setSelected(id), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList filters={filters} onSelect={onSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ filters, onSelect }) => { /* ... */ });
```

`React.memo` does the shallow comparison, but it only helps when the props are actually stable — which `useMemo` and `useCallback` ensure. All three work together.
